       This function is deprecated; use rreeaaddddiirr(3) instead.

       The  rreeaaddddiirr__rr()  function was invented as a reentrant version of rreeaadd‐‐
       ddiirr(3).  It reads the next directory entry from  the  directory  stream
       _d_i_r_p,  and  returns it in the caller‐allocated buffer pointed to by _e_n_‐
       _t_r_y.  For details of the _d_i_r_e_n_t structure, see rreeaaddddiirr(3).

       A pointer to the returned buffer is placed in _*_r_e_s_u_l_t; if  the  end  of
       the  directory stream was encountered, then NULL is instead returned in
       _*_r_e_s_u_l_t.

       It is recommended that applications use  rreeaaddddiirr(3)  instead  of  rreeaadd‐‐
       ddiirr__rr().   Furthermore, since glibc 2.24, glibc deprecates rreeaaddddiirr__rr().
       The reasons are as follows:

       •  On systems where NNAAMMEE__MMAAXX is undefined, calling rreeaaddddiirr__rr()  may  be
          unsafe  because  the  interface does not allow the caller to specify
          the length of the buffer used for the returned directory entry.

       •  On some systems, rreeaaddddiirr__rr() can’t read directory entries with  very
          long  names.   When the glibc implementation encounters such a name,
          rreeaaddddiirr__rr() fails with the error EENNAAMMEETTOOOOLLOONNGG _a_f_t_e_r _t_h_e _f_i_n_a_l _d_i_r_e_c_‐
          _t_o_r_y _e_n_t_r_y _h_a_s _b_e_e_n _r_e_a_d.  On some other  systems,  rreeaaddddiirr__rr()  may
          return  a  success  status, but the returned _d___n_a_m_e field may not be
          null terminated or may be truncated.

       •  In the current POSIX.1 specification (POSIX.1‐2008),  rreeaaddddiirr(3)  is
          not  required to be thread‐safe.  However, in modern implementations
          (including the glibc implementation), concurrent calls to rreeaaddddiirr(3)
          that specify different directory streams  are  thread‐safe.   There‐
          fore,  the  use  of  rreeaaddddiirr__rr()  is generally unnecessary in multi‐
          threaded programs.  In cases where multiple threads must  read  from
          the  same  directory stream, using rreeaaddddiirr(3) with external synchro‐
          nization is still preferable to the use of rreeaaddddiirr__rr(), for the rea‐
          sons given in the points above.

       •  It is expected that a future version  of  POSIX.1  will  make  rreeaadd‐‐
          ddiirr__rr()  obsolete,  and  require that rreeaaddddiirr(3) be thread‐safe when
          concurrently employed on different directory streams.

